Assignment 4: Repository with at Least Three Meaningful Commits
Objective

To create a Git repository, organize HTML and CSS assignment files, and maintain a meaningful commit history using Git.

Step 1: Configure Git

Open your VS Code terminal and run these commands. Replace the name and email with your own Git identity.

git config --global user.name "Shreya Sharma"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"

To verify your configuration, run:

git config --global --list
Step 2: Create a Repository

Navigate to your assignment folder using the terminal.

For example, if you want to create the repository on your Desktop, run:

cd "$HOME\Desktop"
mkdir html-css-assignments
cd html-css-assignments

Initialize Git:

git init
git branch -M main

What do these commands do?

mkdir: Creates a new folder.
cd: Opens the folder in the terminal.
git init: Initializes a Git repository.
git branch -M main: Names the current branch main.
Step 3: Create the First Commit

Create a README file to describe your project.

"# HTML & CSS Assignments" | Out-File -Encoding utf8 README.md

Add the file to Git:

git add README.md

Commit the changes:

git commit -m "docs: add README with project overview"

Commit 1: docs: add README with project overview

Step 4: Add Assignment Files and Create the Second Commit

Copy your assignment files into the html-css-assignments folder.

For example, if your assignment files are stored in another folder, copy them into the repository using File Explorer or the appropriate PowerShell command.

Make sure the files are inside the repository before proceeding.

To check the files, run:

dir

Add your first five assignment files. The following command assumes the filenames match the patterns in your assignment instructions:

git add 01-*.md 02-*.md 03-*.md 04-*.md 05-*.md

Commit the changes:

git commit -m "docs: add setup and git assignments (1-5)"

Commit 2: docs: add setup and git assignments (1-5)

Note: If your assignment files have different names or extensions, use their actual filenames in the git add command.

Step 5: Add HTML Assignments and Create the Third Commit

Add your HTML assignment files.

git add 06-*.html 07-*.html 08-*.html 09-*.html 10-*.html

Commit the changes:

git commit -m "feat: add HTML basics assignments (6-10)"

Commit 3: feat: add HTML basics assignments (6-10)

If your HTML files use different names, replace these patterns with your actual filenames.

Step 6: Verify Your Commit History

Run:

git log --oneline

Your output should look similar to this:

a1b2c3d feat: add HTML basics assignments (6-10)
d4e5f6g docs: add setup and git assignments (1-5)
h7i8j9k docs: add README with project overview

The characters at the beginning of each line represent abbreviated commit IDs. Yours will be different.

This output proves that your repository contains three meaningful commits.

Good Git Commit Message Rules

Follow these rules when creating commits:

Use clear, concise descriptions.
Use prefixes such as feat:, fix:, docs:, and style:.
Describe one logical change per commit.
Use imperative wording, such as add navbar, instead of added navbar.
Expected Result

After completing the assignment, you should have:

A Git repository named html-css-assignments.
A README.md file describing the project.
Your HTML and CSS assignment files organized in the repository.
At least three meaningful commits.
A commit history visible through git log --oneline.
Conclusion

This assignment demonstrates how to create a Git repository, track files, make meaningful commits, and maintain a clear project history. Git helps developers track changes and manage their code throughout the development process.